var google_ads_keyword_intent_default = "# Google Ads Keyword Intent: Informational vs Commercial vs Transactional\r\n\r\nGoogle Ads keyword intent determines what a person is trying to accomplish when they search. Two keywords can contain similar words and still represent very different opportunities for an advertiser.\r\n\r\nUnderstanding **Google Ads keyword intent** helps you decide which searches belong in a campaign, what message the ad should use, which landing page should receive the click, and what conversion action makes sense. It also helps explain why a campaign can generate plenty of traffic but very few useful leads.\r\n\r\nThis guide breaks search intent into informational, commercial and transactional intent, then shows how to use the framework when researching keywords, structuring campaigns and evaluating performance.\r\n\r\n## What is keyword intent in Google Ads?\r\n\r\nKeyword intent is the underlying purpose behind a search. It answers a simple question: **what is the searcher trying to do right now?**\r\n\r\nThe same person can move through several types of intent during a buying journey. Someone may first learn what a service does, then compare providers, and finally search for a specific provider or action.\r\n\r\nFor Google Ads, intent matters because the advertiser is paying for the opportunity created by the search. The goal is not to label every query perfectly. The goal is to understand whether the query represents demand that your offer can satisfy and what experience should follow the click.\r\n\r\nA useful working model is:\r\n\r\n**Informational → Commercial → Transactional**\r\n\r\nThese are practical marketing categories rather than official Google Ads campaign labels. A real search query can also contain mixed intent.\r\n\r\n## Informational search intent\r\n\r\nInformational intent means the searcher is primarily trying to understand something, learn a process, solve a problem or gather information.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n1. What is Google Ads keyword intent\r\n2. How does Google Ads work\r\n3. What is search intent\r\n4. How to choose Google Ads keywords\r\n5. Google Ads conversion tracking explained\r\n\r\nThe person may eventually become a customer, but the query itself does not necessarily show immediate buying intent.\r\n\r\n### When informational keywords make sense in Google Ads\r\n\r\nInformational keywords are not automatically bad keywords. They can make sense when the business has a strong educational funnel, a low cost way to nurture demand, or a clear reason to capture the visitor.\r\n\r\nThey are more difficult to justify when the campaign has a narrow lead generation objective and every click carries a meaningful acquisition cost.\r\n\r\nBefore bidding on an informational query, ask:\r\n\r\n1. Does this search represent a problem my business can solve?\r\n2. Can the landing page answer the question better than competing results?\r\n3. Is there a useful next step that fits the visitor's stage?\r\n4. Can the business afford to pay for early stage demand?\r\n\r\nIf the answer to these questions is weak, the query may belong in organic content rather than paid search.\r\n\r\n## Commercial search intent\r\n\r\nCommercial intent sits closer to a buying decision. The searcher is evaluating options, providers, solutions or prices but may not yet be ready to complete the final action.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n1. Best Google Ads agency in Dubai\r\n2. Google Ads specialist UAE\r\n3. Google Ads management services\r\n4. Google Ads agency reviews\r\n5. Google Ads pricing for small business\r\n\r\nThese searches often indicate that the person is comparing alternatives. The exact query determines whether the best landing experience should emphasise expertise, services, proof, pricing, process or a consultation.\r\n\r\nCommercial keywords can be especially useful for lead generation because they can combine a clear problem with evidence that the searcher is evaluating solutions.\r\n\r\n## Transactional search intent\r\n\r\nTransactional intent indicates that the searcher is closer to taking a specific action. The action could be purchasing, booking, contacting, requesting a quote or choosing a provider.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n1. Hire Google Ads specialist Dubai\r\n2. Book Google Ads consultation\r\n3. Google Ads management agency Dubai\r\n4. Google Ads consultant near me\r\n5. Google Ads audit service\r\n\r\nThe important point is not that every transactional query will convert. Intent is a signal, not a guarantee.\r\n\r\nA transactional search can still fail to produce a lead if the offer is weak, the landing page is mismatched, the price is unsuitable, the business lacks trust or the conversion path creates friction.\r\n\r\n## Informational vs commercial vs transactional keywords\r\n\r\n| Intent | Main question | Typical search behaviour | Suitable landing experience |\r\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\r\n| Informational | What is this or how does it work? | Learning and problem solving | Guide, explanation or educational resource |\r\n| Commercial | Which solution or provider should I choose? | Comparison and evaluation | Service page, comparison page or proof led landing page |\r\n| Transactional | How do I take action now? | Booking, buying or contacting | Focused offer page with a clear conversion path |\r\n\r\nThe categories should guide decisions, not become rigid rules.\r\n\r\n## How to identify search intent from a keyword\r\n\r\nDo not classify a keyword only by one word such as \"best\", \"price\" or \"how\". Read the whole query and consider what the person would expect to see after clicking.\r\n\r\n### Look at the language of the query\r\n\r\nWords such as **what**, **why**, **how**, **guide** and **meaning** often indicate informational intent.\r\n\r\nWords such as **best**, **top**, **reviews**, **comparison**, **agency**, **specialist** and **pricing** can indicate commercial evaluation.\r\n\r\nWords such as **hire**, **book**, **buy**, **quote**, **service** and highly specific provider searches can indicate stronger transactional intent.\r\n\r\nThese patterns are clues rather than rules. A keyword containing \"price\" may still be early research, while a keyword without an obvious buying word can represent strong commercial demand.\r\n\r\n### Study the search results\r\n\r\nThe search results are useful evidence of how Google interprets a query.\r\n\r\nIf the results are dominated by guides, definitions and educational pages, the query may have a strong informational component. If results feature service pages, agencies, product pages or comparison pages, commercial intent may be stronger.\r\n\r\nThis is where **search intent** becomes more useful than keyword wording alone. You are trying to understand the page type and answer type that the search engine believes best satisfies the query.\r\n\r\n### Consider the business model\r\n\r\nIntent cannot be separated completely from the offer.\r\n\r\nA software company may treat an educational query as an opportunity for a free trial. A consultancy may need a much stronger commercial signal before paying for a click. An ecommerce business may find transactional product searches more valuable than broad category research.\r\n\r\nThe same keyword can therefore have different economic value for different businesses.\r\n\r\n## How keyword intent should influence Google Ads campaign structure\r\n\r\nIntent should influence structure when the difference creates a meaningful management decision.\r\n\r\nFor example, a campaign focused on high intent service searches may deserve different messaging and landing pages from a campaign targeting broader problem aware searches.\r\n\r\nAvoid creating dozens of tiny campaigns simply because keywords have slightly different wording. Excessive segmentation can fragment data and make optimisation harder.\r\n\r\nA better principle is:\r\n\r\n**Separate demand when the difference changes the decision. Keep it together when the difference does not.**\r\n\r\nUseful reasons to separate campaigns or themes can include:\r\n\r\n1. Different intent levels\r\n2. Different services\r\n3. Different geographic economics\r\n4. Different budgets\r\n5. Different offers\r\n6. Different landing pages\r\n7. Different business outcomes\r\n\r\n## Match the ad to the intent\r\n\r\nAn ad should continue the conversation started by the search.\r\n\r\nAn informational search may respond to an educational promise. A commercial search may respond to proof, expertise, comparison or a clear service proposition. A transactional search may need a direct offer and obvious next step.\r\n\r\nConsider the difference between these two hypothetical searches:\r\n\r\n**How to improve Google Ads lead quality**\r\n\r\nand\r\n\r\n**Google Ads lead generation agency Dubai**\r\n\r\nThe first search asks for knowledge. The second signals a stronger interest in finding a provider. Sending both users to exactly the same page and using the same message ignores the difference in intent.\r\n\r\n## Match the landing page to the intent\r\n\r\nThe landing page should satisfy the expectation created by the query and ad.\r\n\r\nFor informational intent, the page may need to educate first and offer a relevant next step later.\r\n\r\nFor commercial intent, the page should help the visitor evaluate the business. Clear positioning, process, evidence and relevant service information can reduce uncertainty.\r\n\r\nFor transactional intent, the page should make the desired action easy. The visitor should understand what they are getting, why the next step is worthwhile and what happens after they convert.\r\n\r\nThis is why a generic homepage is not automatically the best Google Ads landing page. A more focused page can provide stronger message match when the query is specific.\r\n\r\n## Use negative keywords to protect intent\r\n\r\nNegative keywords are useful when search terms repeatedly attract traffic that does not fit the campaign's objective.\r\n\r\nCommon examples can include:\r\n\r\n1. Jobs and careers\r\n2. Free tools when the business sells a paid service\r\n3. Courses when the business sells a service\r\n4. Unrelated industries\r\n5. Unsupported locations\r\n6. Irrelevant use cases\r\n\r\nDo not add negatives simply because a query contains an informational word. A query such as \"how to choose a Google Ads specialist\" could still represent valuable commercial research for the right business.\r\n\r\nReview actual search terms and downstream outcomes before making aggressive exclusions.\r\n\r\n## Search intent and match types are not the same thing\r\n\r\nA common mistake is treating keyword match type as if it determines intent.\r\n\r\nMatch types control how closely a user's search can relate to the keyword for eligibility. Intent describes what the user is trying to accomplish.\r\n\r\nThey are connected but not interchangeable.\r\n\r\nA broad match keyword can attract searches across multiple intent patterns. A phrase or exact match keyword can still receive queries with different underlying motivations. That means intent analysis remains necessary even when the account uses tighter matching.\r\n\r\n## How to use keyword intent during keyword research\r\n\r\nA useful keyword research workflow is:\r\n\r\n### Step 1: Start with the business outcome\r\n\r\nDecide what you actually want the campaign to produce. Is it qualified leads, booked consultations, purchases or another measurable action?\r\n\r\n### Step 2: Build the demand universe\r\n\r\nCollect keywords around the problem, service, category, competitor set and commercial use cases.\r\n\r\n### Step 3: Classify intent\r\n\r\nGroup queries into informational, commercial, transactional, irrelevant and mixed intent.\r\n\r\n### Step 4: Check the SERP\r\n\r\nReview the current result types and compare them with your proposed landing page.\r\n\r\n### Step 5: Estimate economic value\r\n\r\nA high intent keyword with expensive clicks is not automatically better than a lower cost keyword. Consider conversion rate, lead quality, close rate, customer value and margin.\r\n\r\n### Step 6: Build the campaign around useful differences\r\n\r\nGroup keywords according to differences that affect ad messaging, landing pages, budgets or optimisation decisions.\r\n\r\n## How to measure whether your intent strategy is working\r\n\r\nDo not judge keyword intent only by click through rate.\r\n\r\nA useful measurement chain is:\r\n\r\n**Impressions → Clicks → Leads → Qualified leads → Opportunities → Customers → Revenue**\r\n\r\nCTR can help diagnose the relationship between the query and ad message. Conversion rate helps evaluate what happens after the click. Cost per qualified lead helps connect acquisition efficiency with lead quality. Customer and revenue data provide the strongest commercial context.\r\n\r\nGoogle Ads currently distinguishes between primary conversion actions, which can be used for bidding when the associated goal is used for optimisation, and secondary actions, which are generally observed rather than used for bidding. Correct configuration matters because automated bidding depends on the conversion signals selected for optimisation. [Google's guidance on primary and secondary conversion actions](https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/11461796) explains the distinction.\r\n\r\nFor lead generation businesses, enhanced conversions for leads can also connect first party lead information with later offline outcomes. Google's current documentation explains that enhanced conversions for leads supplements offline conversion data with hashed customer information and can improve measurement and bidding signals. [Google's enhanced conversions for leads documentation](https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/15713840) provides the current implementation guidance.\r\n\r\n## Common keyword intent mistakes\r\n\r\n### Treating all search traffic as equally valuable\r\n\r\nA click from an early research query and a click from a high intent service query can have very different commercial value.\r\n\r\n### Sending every query to the homepage\r\n\r\nA generic destination can force the visitor to repeat the research they already completed in the search.\r\n\r\n### Assuming high CTR means strong intent\r\n\r\nA compelling ad can attract clicks from people who are interested in the topic but not suitable for the offer.\r\n\r\n### Optimising only for CPL\r\n\r\nLow cost leads can still create a weak business result if qualification and sales conversion are poor.\r\n\r\n### Over segmenting campaigns\r\n\r\nMore campaign structure does not automatically mean more control. If the segmentation does not change a decision, it may only add complexity.\r\n\r\n### Using keyword tools without checking the SERP\r\n\r\nVolume and keyword difficulty do not tell you everything about what the searcher expects. The live search results provide important context.\r\n\r\n## A practical Google Ads keyword intent framework\r\n\r\nWhen reviewing a keyword, ask these questions in order:\r\n\r\n| Question | What you are trying to learn |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| What is the person trying to accomplish? | Underlying intent |\r\n| What does the search result page suggest? | Expected content type |\r\n| Can my business satisfy that intent? | Relevance |\r\n| What would the visitor expect after clicking? | Message and page alignment |\r\n| What action should the visitor take? | Conversion design |\r\n| Is that action economically valuable? | Business value |\r\n| Can I measure the downstream outcome? | Optimisation quality |\r\n\r\nThis framework prevents keyword research from becoming a spreadsheet exercise disconnected from the customer journey.\r\n\r\n## When informational keywords should become content instead of ads\r\n\r\nNot every useful keyword belongs in a paid search campaign.\r\n\r\nIf the search is primarily educational and the visitor is unlikely to take a commercially meaningful action, organic content may be a better acquisition asset. A detailed guide can capture the search, answer the question and introduce the reader to relevant services without forcing a paid click into a conversion path that does not fit the intent.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly useful when informational topics have strong relevance to the broader customer journey. The article can become an entry point, while commercial and transactional pages handle later stage demand.\r\n\r\nThe decision should still be based on economics. If an informational keyword produces valuable customers at an acceptable acquisition cost, there is no rule saying it must be organic only.\r\n\r\n## How a performance marketer should think about keyword intent\r\n\r\nKeyword intent is ultimately a decision framework.\r\n\r\nA strong **Performance Marketing Specialist** does not treat keywords as isolated strings. The keyword is evidence about a person's problem, awareness level and likely next step. That evidence should influence the ad, landing page, conversion action, budget and measurement model. [Explore the wider performance marketing approach](https://ashwinjames.com/).\r\n\r\nThe most useful question is therefore not \"Which keyword has the highest volume?\"\r\n\r\nIt is:\r\n\r\n**Which search represents valuable demand that our business can satisfy better than the alternatives?**\r\n\r\nThat question leads to better campaign structure and better optimisation decisions.\r\n\r\n## FAQ\r\n\r\n### What is keyword intent in Google Ads?\r\n\r\nKeyword intent describes what a person is trying to accomplish with a search. For practical campaign planning, queries can be grouped into informational, commercial, transactional and irrelevant or mixed intent.\r\n\r\n### What is the difference between informational and commercial keywords?\r\n\r\nInformational keywords usually indicate that the person is learning or solving a problem. Commercial keywords usually indicate that the person is evaluating solutions, providers, prices or alternatives.\r\n\r\n### Are transactional keywords always better for Google Ads?\r\n\r\nNo. Transactional intent can be valuable, but the best keyword depends on the business model, economics, competition, offer, conversion rate and downstream customer value.\r\n\r\n### How do I identify commercial keywords?\r\n\r\nLook at the complete query, modifiers such as best, agency, specialist, reviews or pricing, the search results, and the action the visitor would reasonably expect to take after clicking. Commercial intent is a combination of signals rather than one specific word.\r\n\r\n### Should informational keywords be excluded from Google Ads?\r\n\r\nNot automatically. Exclude them when they consistently attract demand that does not support the campaign objective. Keep them when they produce valuable outcomes or support a deliberate education and nurture strategy.\r\n\r\n### Does match type determine search intent?\r\n\r\nNo. Match type affects keyword eligibility and query matching, while intent describes the user's purpose. A campaign can still receive different intent patterns within the same match type.\r\n\r\n## Conclusion\r\n\r\nGoogle Ads keyword intent is useful because it connects keyword research with the actual customer journey.\r\n\r\nInformational searches usually need education. Commercial searches usually need evaluation support. Transactional searches usually need a clear path to action. But these categories are not rigid, and the right decision depends on the business model and economics behind the traffic.\r\n\r\nUse the search query, the SERP, the ad, the landing page and the downstream conversion data together. Then structure campaigns around differences that genuinely change decisions.\r\n\r\nThe objective is not to collect the largest keyword list or generate the most clicks. It is to identify valuable demand, match the experience to that demand and measure whether the resulting traffic creates useful business outcomes.\r\n";
